{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And he shall slaughter the ox.</b> The received tradition of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, is that slaughter is valid by a layman, and therefore it says ושחט in the singular and did not say ושחטו as it said והקריבו and וזרקו in the plural; and it reverted to the singular and said והפשיט את העולה — meaning, the one who slaughtered is the one who skins, whether a Levite or an Israelite, and so it is written (2 Chronicles 35:11): והלוים מפשיטים. And the reason it used the term <b>בקר</b> is that it is a two-year-old, which is why it did not use the term פר or עגל, for עגל is a one-year-old and פר a three-year-old; and likewise everywhere it says כבש or כבשים it is a one-year-old, איל or אילים a two-year-old. And in the chapter 'Four New Year's Days' R. Melekh would say: wherever the Torah says עגל unspecified it is a one-year-old, בן בקר a two-year-old, פר a three-year-old.<br> <b>And in Torat Kohanim:</b> עגל a one-year-old, as it is said (Leviticus 9:3): ועגל וכבש בני שנה; בן בקר two years, as it is said (ibid.): עגל בן בקר לחטאת ואיל לעולה תמימים — it equated בן בקר with איל תמימים also in years, thus far. But a שעיר, which is among the small goats, within a year or at one year old is called שעיר unspecified, and after one year it is called שעיר עזים."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:32.201354327Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}